9. HIGHLIGHTS                                        *parley-highlights*

All highlight groups are defined with `default = true`, so your colorscheme
or manual `vim.api.nvim_set_hl()` calls take precedence.

ParleySign                                           *hl-ParleySign*
    Gutter sign for commented lines.
    Default link: `DiagnosticSignInfo`

ParleyStaleSign                                      *hl-ParleyStaleSign*
    Gutter sign for lines whose anchor has diverged (stale).
    Default link: `Comment`

ParleyVirtualTextMeta                                *hl-ParleyVirtualTextMeta*
    Author and timestamp text below commented lines.
    Default link: `DiagnosticVirtualTextHint`

ParleyStaleVirtualTextMeta                           *hl-ParleyStaleVirtualTextMeta*
    Author and timestamp text for stale comments.
    Default link: `Comment`

ParleyVirtualText                                    *hl-ParleyVirtualText*
    Comment body text below commented lines.
    Default link: `DiagnosticVirtualTextInfo`

ParleyStaleVirtualText                               *hl-ParleyStaleVirtualText*
    Comment body text for stale comments.
    Default link: `Comment`

Example override: >lua
    vim.api.nvim_set_hl(0, "ParleySign", { fg = "#e0af68" })
<

==============================================================================
10. PROVIDERS                                        *parley-providers*

Parley uses a provider abstraction to support multiple hosting platforms.

Local revision reads and new-comment validation use the detected VCS (Git or
Arc). Mapping compares review-head content with a loaded buffer's text, including
unsaved edits, or with the working-tree file when no buffer is loaded. Edits
refresh positions after a short debounce without an API request. Separate
checkouts have independent mappings even when they share the same review.

If content cannot be read, existing locations remain visible as stale
approximations with a warning. Deleted lines have no precise local position;
quickfix entries are marked unavailable rather than targeting the original line.

New comments require local HEAD to match the review revision, a clean file, and
no unsaved changes. Every selected line must belong to the review's changed
new-side lines. Validation runs before composition and again before sending.
Failures preserve the draft; Parley never bypasses a failed VCS check.

Internal VCS helpers take a VcsInfo table rather than a root string. Shared
workflows use DetectedReview.head_sha; write_context remains provider-private.

GitHub                                               *parley-provider-github*

The built-in GitHub provider uses the `gh` CLI for API calls and supports
both github.com and GitHub Enterprise Server (GHES) instances.

Authentication: ~

Tokens are resolved in the following order:

  1. Environment variables:
     - `GH_TOKEN` / `GITHUB_TOKEN` (for github.com and *.ghe.com)
     - `GH_ENTERPRISE_TOKEN` / `GITHUB_ENTERPRISE_TOKEN` (for GHES)
  2. The `oauth_token` field in `~/.config/gh/hosts.yml`

Run `gh auth login` to set up authentication.

Provider options: ~

GitHub-specific options live under `providers.github` in the setup call.
See |parley-configuration|.

Limitations: ~

  - `resolve` / `unresolve` are not supported (require GraphQL).
  - `submit_review` is supported: "approve", "request_changes", "comment".

------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Arcanum                                              *parley-provider-arcanum*

The built-in Arcanum provider supports Arc (Arcadia VCS) repositories via
the Arcanum public REST API at arcanum.yandex.net.

The provider is activated automatically when the current buffer belongs to an
Arc repository (detected via `arc root`).

Authentication: ~

OAuth tokens are resolved in the following order:

  1. Environment variable: `ARCANUM_TOKEN`
  2. File: `~/.arc/token` (plain text, single line)

To obtain a token: https://a.yandex-team.ru/oauth/token

User login detection: ~

The current user login is derived from `arc config -l` output (user.email
field). The login is used to filter PRs authored by the current user and to
determine `is_own` on comments.

Provider options: ~

Arcanum-specific options live under `providers.arcanum` in the setup call: >lua
    require("parley").setup({
      providers = {
        arcanum = {
          timeout_ms = 10000,      -- HTTP request timeout in milliseconds
          retry_count = 2,         -- Number of retries on transient errors
          retry_base_delay_ms = 250,
          retry_max_delay_ms = 2000,
          host = "arcanum.yandex.net",  -- API hostname (rarely changed)
        },
      },
    })
<

Limitations: ~

  - `resolve` / `unresolve` are not supported by the Arcanum public API.
  - `react` (reactions) are not supported by the Arcanum public API.
  - `submit_review` (verdicts) is not supported by the Arcanum public API.
  - Attempting any unsupported operation shows a "not supported" error.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Custom providers: ~

After calling `setup()`, you can register additional providers: >lua
    local registry = require("parley.registry")
    registry.register({
      name = "MyProvider",
      detect = function(vcs_info) ... end,
      factory = function(opts) ... end,
      health = function(context)
        -- Optional: local-only diagnostics; never construct a provider here.
        return { { level = "info", message = "Local diagnostics complete" } }
      end,
    })
<

The provider must implement all methods defined in `parley.Provider`.
See `lua/parley/provider.lua` for the full interface.

Provider-owned cache identity: ~

Every provider must implement `provider:cache_identity()`. It runs in a Plenary
coroutine and returns { provider, host, repository, account }, with four nonempty
strings, or nil when stable local credential identity is unavailable. `account`
must be a non-secret fingerprint; never return credentials or include them in
errors. Identity derivation belongs under `lua/parley/providers/` and must not
make network requests.

Parley validates provider construction and identity before publishing provider
snapshots. Missing methods and malformed values are contract errors. Nil identity
keeps live review operations available using isolated temporary state and skips
all persistent review-cache reads, writes, and invalidation.

Review cache keys use the reviews-v2 namespace and hashes of ordered identity
fields and typed branch/PR identifiers. Provider, host, repository, and credential
contexts are isolated. Token rotation causes a cold refresh. Checkout-specific
local mappings remain separate even when remote cache identity is shared.

Legacy review-cache entries are left untouched and never used as a fallback.
The first refresh after this change fetches fresh data. Identity changes during
fetching discard those results and schedule a refresh with the current identity.

`registry.resolve_with_opts(vcs_info)` returns the validated provider and its
opaque detection options. `registry.resolve(vcs_info)` retains its provider-only
return value. Shared cache code consumes validated snapshot identity, not factory
options or private provider fields.

The optional provider-spec `health(context)` callback runs in a plenary.async
coroutine. It receives `vcs_info`, the `opts` returned by detection, and current
`config`. Return a list of `{ level, message }` entries, where level is `ok`,
`info`, `warn`, or `error`. Keep diagnostics local: no API requests, provider
construction, or credentials in messages. A missing callback is informational.
Concrete diagnostic implementations belong under `lua/parley/providers/`.


Provider-owned reactions: ~

Providers may implement these optional, synchronous, local-only methods:

  reaction_choices(review, comment)
      Returns an ordered list of { reaction, label, emoji? } and an optional
      unavailable-reason string. `reaction` is an opaque provider identifier.
      An empty list disables mutation and displays the reason when requested.

  reaction_presentation(reaction_type)
      Returns { label, emoji? } for an existing reaction identifier. Shared UI
      adds counts and viewer state. Unknown identifiers should retain their
      original value as the label.

These methods use the usual provider:method(...) calling convention. Define
concrete catalogs and presentation logic under `lua/parley/providers/`.
Missing methods preserve raw labels and make interactive mutations unavailable;
there is no implicit GitHub catalog. Cached reaction data remains unchanged.

GitHub retains its existing reaction choices and emoji. Arcanum reactions remain
readable using their original codes; changing them is not implemented in Parley.
The picker and direct reaction service reject choices that are not currently
offered. Changing review/provider context while the picker is open requires
reopening it before submitting.

Custom VCS implementations: ~

After `setup()`, register both a detector and an adapter via `parley.vcs`.
Concrete VCS modules belong under `lua/parley/providers/`; a module can be
reused by multiple hosting providers. For example: >lua
    local vcs = require("parley.vcs")
    local custom = require("parley.providers.my_vcs")
    vcs.register_detector("my-vcs", custom.detect)
    vcs.register_adapter("my-vcs", custom.adapter)
<
The detector runs in a plenary.async coroutine and returns a `parley.VcsInfo`
with `vcs = "my-vcs"`, or nil when the path is not supported.

The adapter must provide these functions (called without a `self` argument):

  head()                  Command argument array to read the current revision
  show(revision, path)    Command argument array to read immutable file contents
  status(path)            Command argument array to inspect file status
  dirty(output)           Parse status stdout: true if dirty, false if clean,
                          or nil plus an error string if parsing fails
  diff(base, head, path)  Command argument array producing a zero-context
                          unified review diff, with the review head on new side

Commands run asynchronously in the repository root. Adapter registration rejects
empty names, duplicate names, and missing/non-function methods. Adapter names
must match `VcsInfo.vcs`; unknown names fail without executing commands.

`vcs.reset_adapters()` removes adapters without changing detectors.
`vcs.reset_detectors()` removes detectors without changing adapters.
Every `setup()` resets both and registers built-ins, checking Arc before Git.
Re-register custom implementations after each setup. Direct revision/validation
calls before setup require explicit adapter registration.

==============================================================================
11. HEALTH CHECK                                     *parley-health*

Run: >vim
    :checkhealth parley
<

The health check verifies:

  - Neovim version (>= 0.10) and `plenary.async` availability
  - Configuration state and cache-directory accessibility
  - Optional integrations (telescope.nvim, render-markdown.nvim)
  - The source file's repository using registered VCS detectors
  - Local diagnostics supplied by the matching provider

GitHub diagnostics check Git/gh executables, branch, and credentials from the
environment or hosts.yml. Keyring credentials are not checked. Arcanum diagnostics
check Arc/curl executables, remote branch, login, and local token availability.
Missing executables for unrelated providers do not produce warnings.

Credential availability does not verify server authentication. No API requests or
PR detection are performed. Tokens are never included in built-in diagnostics.

When :checkhealth opens its report buffer, Parley uses the regular alternate
buffer as the source file. If unavailable, repository diagnostics are skipped.
Repository checks also require setup() and Plenary; checks time out after ten
seconds, and late results are discarded. Providers without diagnostic callbacks
and repositories without matching providers are reported informationally.

